    "result_text": "The full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe locality derivation now prefers the postcode. I made two changes:\n\n**`Locality.java`** \u2014 added a postcode-first overload and a lookup helper:\n- `of(String city, String postcode)` \u2014 returns the region whose range contains the postcode, else falls back to the existing city-to-region table via `of(city)`.\n- `regionForPostcode(String postcode)` \u2014 returns the region for a 4-digit postcode in a known range (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099), or `null` when the postcode is absent, non-4-digit, or in no known range.\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `locality` mapping now calls `Locality.of(owner.getCity(), owner.getPostcode())` instead of the city-only form.\n\nBehavior: known cities with an in-range postcode return the same region as before (`coreRegionFromPostcodeRange` \u2014 Sydney/2000 \u2192 NSW), while an unknown city with an in-range postcode is now disambiguated by postcode (`functionalityPostcodePreferredOverCity` \u2014 unknown city/3000 \u2192 VIC).\n\nI left `CheckOwnerPostcode` untouched on purpose: it validates a postcode *against the city's* region, and deriving the region from the postcode there would make the check self-referential and always pass.\n\nTests: `AcceptanceTest` both pass; full suite is `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
